answer-1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64" :answer/id #uuid "1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-112f0d49-eac0-53f1-b261-60430952122b" :activity/id #uuid "112f0d49-eac0-53f1-b261-60430952122b" :activity/title "Matrix Exponentials" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050054 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-9ec79b7a-cc5f-545d-8ccd-c3332ae4a103" "step-19c67ca8-9e4d-5139-bd8f-2436f33a9636" "step-1afee1ae-ae64-51cb-8c32-31c1402e74ce" "step-993f1022-e61a-56df-acc0-e37f356188c9" "step-420af24a-40fb-5dd3-9845-8b85ad169b4c"] :activity/first-step "step-9ec79b7a-cc5f-545d-8ccd-c3332ae4a103" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9ec79b7a-cc5f-545d-8ccd-c3332ae4a103" :step/id #uuid "9ec79b7a-cc5f-545d-8ccd-c3332ae4a103" :step/content 17592186221933 :step/next "step-19c67ca8-9e4d-5139-bd8f-2436f33a9636" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-19c67ca8-9e4d-5139-bd8f-2436f33a9636" :step/id #uuid "19c67ca8-9e4d-5139-bd8f-2436f33a9636" :step/content 17592186308656 :step/next "step-1afee1ae-ae64-51cb-8c32-31c1402e74ce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1afee1ae-ae64-51cb-8c32-31c1402e74ce" :step/id #uuid "1afee1ae-ae64-51cb-8c32-31c1402e74ce" :step/content 17592186222002 :step/next "step-993f1022-e61a-56df-acc0-e37f356188c9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-993f1022-e61a-56df-acc0-e37f356188c9" :step/id #uuid "993f1022-e61a-56df-acc0-e37f356188c9" :step/content 17592186308673 :step/next "step-420af24a-40fb-5dd3-9845-8b85ad169b4c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-420af24a-40fb-5dd3-9845-8b85ad169b4c" :step/id #uuid "420af24a-40fb-5dd3-9845-8b85ad169b4c" :step/content "question-5f681488-76a6-582b-8440-bda618691a45" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac" :question/id #uuid "63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-107114" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b,c\\in R\\}.$    \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an element of $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =k\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a & 2b \\\\ 2c & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under scalar  multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a4c1e3e7-b7eb-5780-ac9f-e4101be00453"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a4c1e3e7-b7eb-5780-ac9f-e4101be00453" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c1e3e7-b7eb-5780-ac9f-e4101be00453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-423e1384-7818-5410-95a8-d0d39c518bd3" "answer-f4920f18-c1a5-58fd-9b3e-acfe48313d6a" "answer-6c462f0d-0366-5361-9779-d3c8686baa44" "answer-0f4db8f3-59c2-52bd-bbc6-7b8fc5889531" "answer-a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-423e1384-7818-5410-95a8-d0d39c518bd3" :answer/id #uuid "423e1384-7818-5410-95a8-d0d39c518bd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4920f18-c1a5-58fd-9b3e-acfe48313d6a" :answer/id #uuid "f4920f18-c1a5-58fd-9b3e-acfe48313d6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c462f0d-0366-5361-9779-d3c8686baa44" :answer/id #uuid "6c462f0d-0366-5361-9779-d3c8686baa44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f4db8f3-59c2-52bd-bbc6-7b8fc5889531" :answer/id #uuid "0f4db8f3-59c2-52bd-bbc6-7b8fc5889531" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88" :answer/id #uuid "a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-be93fcdf-31d3-5e82-b4bf-9e59c7d6464d" :activity/id #uuid "be93fcdf-31d3-5e82-b4bf-9e59c7d6464d" :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050453 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-63ba8bbb-db34-560b-835a-80c6cbe5aee6" "step-bb14753a-b718-5bb4-8590-d7cdea5611b1" "step-b5b4ede0-23d1-5e23-9ac1-9e7f542f0119" "step-2f479215-92de-59bc-a9f7-99164bc800e7" "step-682539ca-dd28-5486-a289-41f166f6fb31"] :activity/first-step "step-63ba8bbb-db34-560b-835a-80c6cbe5aee6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-63ba8bbb-db34-560b-835a-80c6cbe5aee6" :step/id #uuid "63ba8bbb-db34-560b-835a-80c6cbe5aee6" :step/content 17592186125155 :step/next "step-bb14753a-b718-5bb4-8590-d7cdea5611b1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bb14753a-b718-5bb4-8590-d7cdea5611b1" :step/id #uuid "bb14753a-b718-5bb4-8590-d7cdea5611b1" :step/content 17592186125157 :step/next "step-b5b4ede0-23d1-5e23-9ac1-9e7f542f0119" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b5b4ede0-23d1-5e23-9ac1-9e7f542f0119" :step/id #uuid "b5b4ede0-23d1-5e23-9ac1-9e7f542f0119" :step/content 17592186329725 :step/next "step-2f479215-92de-59bc-a9f7-99164bc800e7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2f479215-92de-59bc-a9f7-99164bc800e7" :step/id #uuid "2f479215-92de-59bc-a9f7-99164bc800e7" :step/content 17592186360466 :step/next "step-682539ca-dd28-5486-a289-41f166f6fb31" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-682539ca-dd28-5486-a289-41f166f6fb31" :step/id #uuid "682539ca-dd28-5486-a289-41f166f6fb31" :step/content "question-63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2da34519-4949-5242-90ca-b17d2dfc3fef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2da34519-4949-5242-90ca-b17d2dfc3fef" :question/id #uuid "2da34519-4949-5242-90ca-b17d2dfc3fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-257196" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8xy-4x+y \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+x-6y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-4x+y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}+x-6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy-4x+y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+x-6y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8y-4 & 8x+1 \\\\ 8x+1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y-4 & 8x+1 \\\\ 8x+1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy-4x+y \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+x-6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y-4 & 8x+1 \\\\ 8x+1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy-4x+y \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+x-6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8(2)-4 & 8(1)+1 \\\\ 8(1)+1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8(1)(2)-4(1)+2 \\\\ 4(1)^{2}+(1)-6(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 9 \\\\ 9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{12\\cdot (-6)-9\\cdot 9}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{153}\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -210\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.863 \\\\ 0.627\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9722e690-313b-51ec-99a5-45188eea87c3" "answer-field-e8f36341-6eac-5006-97d5-0fb0b1cafe2c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9722e690-313b-51ec-99a5-45188eea87c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9722e690-313b-51ec-99a5-45188eea87c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e18ea92b-8b23-5b40-aa57-2f8661de8065" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e18ea92b-8b23-5b40-aa57-2f8661de8065"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e18ea92b-8b23-5b40-aa57-2f8661de8065" :answer/id #uuid "e18ea92b-8b23-5b40-aa57-2f8661de8065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.863" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8f36341-6eac-5006-97d5-0fb0b1cafe2c" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f36341-6eac-5006-97d5-0fb0b1cafe2c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dd885b5a-0605-540b-8aed-fbf980d5a010" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd885b5a-0605-540b-8aed-fbf980d5a010"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd885b5a-0605-540b-8aed-fbf980d5a010" :answer/id #uuid "dd885b5a-0605-540b-8aed-fbf980d5a010" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.627" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9dfd0945-4d3e-538e-b613-c3c2e6b0d141" :activity/id #uuid "9dfd0945-4d3e-538e-b613-c3c2e6b0d141" :activity/title "Newton's Method for Optimizing Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048637 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-771bc9ee-acef-593c-92c0-5a668403e45d" "step-a7f21ffd-152a-5b80-9a81-6831dc018252" "step-16446d00-e5b5-5d9b-913d-c5ae0ea22dc7" "step-02f70f84-4879-5483-8c29-2a30eb0b7266" "step-52f3de00-758e-50c2-92d0-b7b2f32051cf"] :activity/first-step "step-771bc9ee-acef-593c-92c0-5a668403e45d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-771bc9ee-acef-593c-92c0-5a668403e45d" :step/id #uuid "771bc9ee-acef-593c-92c0-5a668403e45d" :step/content 17592186204510 :step/next "step-a7f21ffd-152a-5b80-9a81-6831dc018252" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a7f21ffd-152a-5b80-9a81-6831dc018252" :step/id #uuid "a7f21ffd-152a-5b80-9a81-6831dc018252" :step/content 17592186204512 :step/next "step-16446d00-e5b5-5d9b-913d-c5ae0ea22dc7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-16446d00-e5b5-5d9b-913d-c5ae0ea22dc7" :step/id #uuid "16446d00-e5b5-5d9b-913d-c5ae0ea22dc7" :step/content 17592186292591 :step/next "step-02f70f84-4879-5483-8c29-2a30eb0b7266" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-02f70f84-4879-5483-8c29-2a30eb0b7266" :step/id #uuid "02f70f84-4879-5483-8c29-2a30eb0b7266" :step/content 17592186292592 :step/next "step-52f3de00-758e-50c2-92d0-b7b2f32051cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-52f3de00-758e-50c2-92d0-b7b2f32051cf" :step/id #uuid "52f3de00-758e-50c2-92d0-b7b2f32051cf" :step/content "question-2da34519-4949-5242-90ca-b17d2dfc3fef" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-57b49f13-0a2a-5f52-9cc1-6ee2c2a0903e" "question-728b8816-964d-5b2c-b1e2-928105ec3a03" "question-22134247-39ad-5cd2-bebd-e89dbb4b1f1b" "question-b7d1bf90-ec36-5fa3-9648-a21ed4abb91b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-57b49f13-0a2a-5f52-9cc1-6ee2c2a0903e" :question/id #uuid "57b49f13-0a2a-5f52-9cc1-6ee2c2a0903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130710" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x+y+2)\\text{ }i+(-2y+x+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1)+2)\\text{ }i+(-2(-1)+1+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b0633ad-b5b5-5018-885c-c9378c08481e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b0633ad-b5b5-5018-885c-c9378c08481e" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0633ad-b5b5-5018-885c-c9378c08481e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2409f922-d37b-52c8-b658-31fce748e2ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35d25c51-dfcd-51fc-a6c5-cfba7faf93c8" "answer-2409f922-d37b-52c8-b658-31fce748e2ba" "answer-98cc5321-69a6-5c55-bb35-e3e0990fcf78" "answer-298df80d-8ba7-5971-a5be-5f47ba173aaa" "answer-872f1d91-3616-5885-a3fb-55ee0f8eeaa2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35d25c51-dfcd-51fc-a6c5-cfba7faf93c8" :answer/id #uuid "35d25c51-dfcd-51fc-a6c5-cfba7faf93c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2409f922-d37b-52c8-b658-31fce748e2ba" :answer/id #uuid "2409f922-d37b-52c8-b658-31fce748e2ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98cc5321-69a6-5c55-bb35-e3e0990fcf78" :answer/id #uuid "98cc5321-69a6-5c55-bb35-e3e0990fcf78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-298df80d-8ba7-5971-a5be-5f47ba173aaa" :answer/id #uuid "298df80d-8ba7-5971-a5be-5f47ba173aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-872f1d91-3616-5885-a3fb-55ee0f8eeaa2" :answer/id #uuid "872f1d91-3616-5885-a3fb-55ee0f8eeaa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-728b8816-964d-5b2c-b1e2-928105ec3a03" :question/id #uuid "728b8816-964d-5b2c-b1e2-928105ec3a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-129910" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-2y)\\text{ }i+(2y-2x+8)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =(2(2)-2(-2))\\text{ }i+(2(-2)-2(2)+8)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{8\\,i}{\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3867fe6c-1021-55a8-96ee-bf07851c7253"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3867fe6c-1021-55a8-96ee-bf07851c7253" :answer-field/id #uuid "3867fe6c-1021-55a8-96ee-bf07851c7253" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0369593a-3f7e-5fb8-b29d-ae4a507e9a3c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5a5d6a5-c65c-525b-a225-edbf38f69769" "answer-ff481663-9345-5ec5-a52f-457045dd7b64" "answer-a4881f5d-4c74-5ce0-a573-e88708e1aff5" "answer-500271bb-f899-5f15-8b03-04f59f53cf49" "answer-0369593a-3f7e-5fb8-b29d-ae4a507e9a3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5a5d6a5-c65c-525b-a225-edbf38f69769" :answer/id #uuid "f5a5d6a5-c65c-525b-a225-edbf38f69769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff481663-9345-5ec5-a52f-457045dd7b64" :answer/id #uuid "ff481663-9345-5ec5-a52f-457045dd7b64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,i+j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4881f5d-4c74-5ce0-a573-e88708e1aff5" :answer/id #uuid "a4881f5d-4c74-5ce0-a573-e88708e1aff5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-500271bb-f899-5f15-8b03-04f59f53cf49" :answer/id #uuid "500271bb-f899-5f15-8b03-04f59f53cf49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i-j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0369593a-3f7e-5fb8-b29d-ae4a507e9a3c" :answer/id #uuid "0369593a-3f7e-5fb8-b29d-ae4a507e9a3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22134247-39ad-5cd2-bebd-e89dbb4b1f1b" :question/id #uuid "22134247-39ad-5cd2-bebd-e89dbb4b1f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71384" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-(-1))\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-2)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-1)}{‖∇f(2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6670180-2250-5bbf-88e5-1ac41691a6d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6670180-2250-5bbf-88e5-1ac41691a6d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6670180-2250-5bbf-88e5-1ac41691a6d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b333ea2a-2c2c-5da6-9892-bd490c4e153c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db7d5bf9-08cf-5bfc-bf08-aa6568808a73" "answer-95e2877d-3409-5570-b9e1-5b49b7a7040b" "answer-032c2757-efa9-5509-bcca-3daaadba1e52" "answer-b333ea2a-2c2c-5da6-9892-bd490c4e153c" "answer-7d92482e-2f72-52fc-9059-c074d418b128"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db7d5bf9-08cf-5bfc-bf08-aa6568808a73" :answer/id #uuid "db7d5bf9-08cf-5bfc-bf08-aa6568808a73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e2877d-3409-5570-b9e1-5b49b7a7040b" :answer/id #uuid "95e2877d-3409-5570-b9e1-5b49b7a7040b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(i+2j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-032c2757-efa9-5509-bcca-3daaadba1e52" :answer/id #uuid "032c2757-efa9-5509-bcca-3daaadba1e52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i+j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b333ea2a-2c2c-5da6-9892-bd490c4e153c" :answer/id #uuid "b333ea2a-2c2c-5da6-9892-bd490c4e153c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i-j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d92482e-2f72-52fc-9059-c074d418b128" :answer/id #uuid "7d92482e-2f72-52fc-9059-c074d418b128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7d1bf90-ec36-5fa3-9648-a21ed4abb91b" :question/id #uuid "b7d1bf90-ec36-5fa3-9648-a21ed4abb91b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71382" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c522a1b1-6aa4-5c25-9679-9d75080dc4e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c522a1b1-6aa4-5c25-9679-9d75080dc4e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "c522a1b1-6aa4-5c25-9679-9d75080dc4e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-389b4032-f597-5b8b-9fd1-e1313e2896af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df593630-ccc7-5531-a86e-3a99b075f8f7" "answer-3bb26b51-4f96-5040-bc73-852a85ed3afb" "answer-389b4032-f597-5b8b-9fd1-e1313e2896af" "answer-2d9fb5d0-4e4d-56ab-80c9-093d2e1af62f" "answer-118335a9-3f2f-5e77-8049-daf71b5ec146"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df593630-ccc7-5531-a86e-3a99b075f8f7" :answer/id #uuid "df593630-ccc7-5531-a86e-3a99b075f8f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bb26b51-4f96-5040-bc73-852a85ed3afb" :answer/id #uuid "3bb26b51-4f96-5040-bc73-852a85ed3afb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389b4032-f597-5b8b-9fd1-e1313e2896af" :answer/id #uuid "389b4032-f597-5b8b-9fd1-e1313e2896af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d9fb5d0-4e4d-56ab-80c9-093d2e1af62f" :answer/id #uuid "2d9fb5d0-4e4d-56ab-80c9-093d2e1af62f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(i-j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-118335a9-3f2f-5e77-8049-daf71b5ec146" :answer/id #uuid "118335a9-3f2f-5e77-8049-daf71b5ec146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ff3d7f48-7543-5dd7-9bd4-9af3c29d885b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff3d7f48-7543-5dd7-9bd4-9af3c29d885b" :question/id #uuid "ff3d7f48-7543-5dd7-9bd4-9af3c29d885b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131118" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(2x+{x}^{2}yz){e}^{xyz}\\,i+({x}^{3}z{e}^{xyz}+2y)\\,j+{x}^{3}y{e}^{xyz}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =(2(1)+({1}^{2})(-2)(0)){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,i+(({1}^{3})(0){e}^{1(-2)(0)}+2(-2))\\,j+({1}^{3})(-2){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22190f85-7ea5-5d9f-80dd-195c9acabba5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22190f85-7ea5-5d9f-80dd-195c9acabba5" :answer-field/id #uuid "22190f85-7ea5-5d9f-80dd-195c9acabba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6a6833e3-d101-5239-8525-af4ff8b4fdaf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55dd2830-6432-52c0-a771-384b6364ede6" "answer-7a765b73-bb6d-588d-b943-9033158431d0" "answer-6a6833e3-d101-5239-8525-af4ff8b4fdaf" "answer-f762dc04-a888-5706-8878-b795fb0dc805" "answer-ca7234d8-3e14-5c71-b5b0-e92152c1bf9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55dd2830-6432-52c0-a771-384b6364ede6" :answer/id #uuid "55dd2830-6432-52c0-a771-384b6364ede6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a765b73-bb6d-588d-b943-9033158431d0" :answer/id #uuid "7a765b73-bb6d-588d-b943-9033158431d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a6833e3-d101-5239-8525-af4ff8b4fdaf" :answer/id #uuid "6a6833e3-d101-5239-8525-af4ff8b4fdaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f762dc04-a888-5706-8878-b795fb0dc805" :answer/id #uuid "f762dc04-a888-5706-8878-b795fb0dc805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,i-j-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca7234d8-3e14-5c71-b5b0-e92152c1bf9d" :answer/id #uuid "ca7234d8-3e14-5c71-b5b0-e92152c1bf9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j+4\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e8fb1410-51ee-5a0a-9a08-61072eaa2e45"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8fb1410-51ee-5a0a-9a08-61072eaa2e45" :question/id #uuid "e8fb1410-51ee-5a0a-9a08-61072eaa2e45" :question/math-academy-id "q-225260" :question/problem "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$-12{a}^{2}-6a+6=$\n{{field-1}}$(a\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)($\n{{field-3}}\n$a\\,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-12{a}^{2}-6a+6\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-6.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(2{a}^{2}+a-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{a}^{2}+a-1$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times (-1)=-2$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $1$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-2$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(-1,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,2)$ add to $1:$ \n\n$$\n-1+2=1\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-a+2a,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}^{2}+a-1 & = \\\\ 2{a}^{2}-a+2a-1 & = \\\\ (2{a}^{2}-a)+(2a-1) & = \\\\ a(2a-1)+(2a-1) & = \\\\ (a+1)(2a-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12{a}^{2}-6a+6 & = \\\\ -6(2{a}^{2}+a-1) & = \\\\ -6(a+1)(2a-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1743a921-81e3-55b5-9d48-3a7a62d7fca6" "answer-field-417b571f-db4b-5ef9-8922-9b03f5302044" "answer-field-97e49246-be20-50e1-b49e-21fb6572d864"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1743a921-81e3-55b5-9d48-3a7a62d7fca6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1743a921-81e3-55b5-9d48-3a7a62d7fca6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b980c429-3941-5497-9718-eff2d9e2f790" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b980c429-3941-5497-9718-eff2d9e2f790"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b980c429-3941-5497-9718-eff2d9e2f790" :answer/id #uuid "b980c429-3941-5497-9718-eff2d9e2f790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-417b571f-db4b-5ef9-8922-9b03f5302044" :answer-field/id #uuid "417b571f-db4b-5ef9-8922-9b03f5302044" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-592954bd-e4e3-532e-b6ca-f2133054c745" :answer-field/choices ["answer-592954bd-e4e3-532e-b6ca-f2133054c745"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-592954bd-e4e3-532e-b6ca-f2133054c745" :answer/id #uuid "592954bd-e4e3-532e-b6ca-f2133054c745" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97e49246-be20-50e1-b49e-21fb6572d864" :answer-field/id #uuid "97e49246-be20-50e1-b49e-21fb6572d864" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fc2978a6-8ff8-5d5b-8005-22bef1697c15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc2978a6-8ff8-5d5b-8005-22bef1697c15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc2978a6-8ff8-5d5b-8005-22bef1697c15" :answer/id #uuid "fc2978a6-8ff8-5d5b-8005-22bef1697c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054604 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03d1d0b4-f385-5136-a615-f2e1e9fef632"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-03d1d0b4-f385-5136-a615-f2e1e9fef632" :question/id #uuid "03d1d0b4-f385-5136-a615-f2e1e9fef632" :question/math-academy-id "q-338386" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+6x+9)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+6x+9)^{3}} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}+6x+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}+6x+9))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3(0)^{2}+6(0)+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0+0+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{729} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b99fd557-903d-52f2-b885-ba116704ea23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b99fd557-903d-52f2-b885-ba116704ea23" :answer-field/id #uuid "b99fd557-903d-52f2-b885-ba116704ea23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fcc5bc51-ebe5-5f7b-8b5e-1457adb75f01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fcc5bc51-ebe5-5f7b-8b5e-1457adb75f01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcc5bc51-ebe5-5f7b-8b5e-1457adb75f01" :answer/id #uuid "fcc5bc51-ebe5-5f7b-8b5e-1457adb75f01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115323 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7c7074e0-f02c-5273-a98a-de3d3f3ee6a7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c7074e0-f02c-5273-a98a-de3d3f3ee6a7" :question/id #uuid "7c7074e0-f02c-5273-a98a-de3d3f3ee6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-76083" :question/problem "What is the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $3\\le 4x+7\\le 19$ or $6x+8<2?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by solving $3\\le 4x+7\\le 19:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & \\le 4x+7\\le 19 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4x\\le 12 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d7303c5608dbd17911794959438f925d7ab6edd3ebba600d657b396ccf8eaa9.png)\n\nThen, we solve $6x+8<2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+8 & <2 \\\\ 6x & <-6 \\\\ x & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad5692ee92c2b67f50c0354020642bd9c711bdf7b88f4d476455512e33f9fc5f.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/63/638b44af1e33b78102a0dbfa3857c1292f200e026f54adf92fe77a97bf7e10a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2baf93f4-97eb-565b-8259-f33d93d8df90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2baf93f4-97eb-565b-8259-f33d93d8df90" :answer-field/id #uuid "2baf93f4-97eb-565b-8259-f33d93d8df90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23f86dbc-d003-51d1-827e-f13bf7fdff1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fad71dcb-4bdd-5e5e-9ebe-bbb5c3d95d50" "answer-23f86dbc-d003-51d1-827e-f13bf7fdff1f" "answer-45465aa1-5e0a-5908-b63c-595a84123bb3" "answer-d5ac308f-1478-5136-85b9-dc1b3ab87104" "answer-982998cd-20b3-5f6f-b36c-446e47274019"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fad71dcb-4bdd-5e5e-9ebe-bbb5c3d95d50" :answer/id #uuid "fad71dcb-4bdd-5e5e-9ebe-bbb5c3d95d50" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/24/2463e5d528a3af7798456523c4e456c102ee3fb07828cd5680180d75bb27d2fd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23f86dbc-d003-51d1-827e-f13bf7fdff1f" :answer/id #uuid "23f86dbc-d003-51d1-827e-f13bf7fdff1f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/63/638b44af1e33b78102a0dbfa3857c1292f200e026f54adf92fe77a97bf7e10a1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45465aa1-5e0a-5908-b63c-595a84123bb3" :answer/id #uuid "45465aa1-5e0a-5908-b63c-595a84123bb3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7d/7d7303c5608dbd17911794959438f925d7ab6edd3ebba600d657b396ccf8eaa9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5ac308f-1478-5136-85b9-dc1b3ab87104" :answer/id #uuid "d5ac308f-1478-5136-85b9-dc1b3ab87104" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a6/a63dd45bee5f32ae88bbb8fbfc274def0539794cfc3b1e88a34a42583832ef43.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-982998cd-20b3-5f6f-b36c-446e47274019" :answer/id #uuid "982998cd-20b3-5f6f-b36c-446e47274019" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ad/ad5692ee92c2b67f50c0354020642bd9c711bdf7b88f4d476455512e33f9fc5f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125027 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059638 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7002a70c-d40e-53ed-9d27-c94492760a6a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7002a70c-d40e-53ed-9d27-c94492760a6a" :question/id #uuid "7002a70c-d40e-53ed-9d27-c94492760a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-22036" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a36fe195458e459aea993232a72ed21e0c780135dc4dbe4bece45bfc3b6e50e2.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $50+25\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=50+25\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 50+25\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 25(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 25(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 25\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 25 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-063ce151-0ba3-5c75-910b-b7e2c256e6ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-063ce151-0ba3-5c75-910b-b7e2c256e6ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "063ce151-0ba3-5c75-910b-b7e2c256e6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad072040-2706-54b1-aafe-7b97f68a33a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad072040-2706-54b1-aafe-7b97f68a33a2" "answer-2272b44c-dbba-5930-a68e-ca0f3289afd9" "answer-a30e18e9-8725-50fa-90c2-6a03a46e5e68" "answer-015e29d4-44ac-5ca8-a43f-99ecdd54ff48" "answer-29d41b66-493d-5b0a-96b8-072a9c66c207"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad072040-2706-54b1-aafe-7b97f68a33a2" :answer/id #uuid "ad072040-2706-54b1-aafe-7b97f68a33a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2272b44c-dbba-5930-a68e-ca0f3289afd9" :answer/id #uuid "2272b44c-dbba-5930-a68e-ca0f3289afd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a30e18e9-8725-50fa-90c2-6a03a46e5e68" :answer/id #uuid "a30e18e9-8725-50fa-90c2-6a03a46e5e68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-015e29d4-44ac-5ca8-a43f-99ecdd54ff48" :answer/id #uuid "015e29d4-44ac-5ca8-a43f-99ecdd54ff48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29d41b66-493d-5b0a-96b8-072a9c66c207" :answer/id #uuid "29d41b66-493d-5b0a-96b8-072a9c66c207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054664 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a422bda1-83e5-5ea1-b276-7fb095067ffb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a422bda1-83e5-5ea1-b276-7fb095067ffb" :question/id #uuid "a422bda1-83e5-5ea1-b276-7fb095067ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-78760" :question/problem "![](images/61/612a4d26c399f754318b7aceb2bb0f329cf9515ea49a7da0d84d5d4aa1b672ea.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-5 \\\\ y &=-{x}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9da50ac2c05ae16125d48ed96e5a7a7ed1dbc03ddb53e96ab301d502abb17f.png)\n\nWe see from the graph that the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-3,-5)\\,$ and $\\,(3,-5).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-70c3d0a0-cfc9-5368-8e0e-665d3b3527ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-70c3d0a0-cfc9-5368-8e0e-665d3b3527ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "70c3d0a0-cfc9-5368-8e0e-665d3b3527ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c967535-4ab0-58a8-a729-8bc0b2f84aea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-228618c5-7982-